00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.730
With this, let's get started.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:54.220
So when we talk about
a hybrid environment,

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.290
we're specifically thinking
this very picture, right.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.740
You have a set up
somewhere, be it on-prem,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.560
be it some other cloud, and
set up in the cloud like GCP.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.419
And what you really
want to do is

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:15.490
to be able to run your
workloads in the same way

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.860
and monitor them
in the same way.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.300
So ideally, that would be
the very same monitoring

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.500
and logging solutions with
the very same dashboards

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.270
and very same alerts that
you would apply here, here

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.600
meaning in a cloud,
and on premises

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.110
so that you can monitor
and see what's happening.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.710
But even more convenient would
be a situation like that,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.880
where you have just one point
in which you can compare

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.300
how your system is doing,
which is specifically important

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.850
if you're running the
workloads with some spill

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.130
over between one and the
other, and you really

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.930
want to understand
what's happening.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.620
More about it in a
demo in a second.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.560
All right, so
before we go there,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.450
just realize Kubernetes brought
this enormous opportunity

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.910
of doing exactly
this, because the way

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.390
of obstructing your
workloads on top

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.490
of the physical
infrastructure lets

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.530
you look at your deployments
and your workloads

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.940
in the exact same way.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.440
So how does the data flow
from the darkest corners

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.340
of Kubernetes?

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.400
Let me get you a very
quick walk through.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.810
First, Kubernetes
per se does not

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.460
have any sophisticated built in
monitoring and logging system.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.840
However, many components depend
on monitoring data, right,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.780
so things like Horizontal
Pod Autoscaling, scheduler,

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.240
kubectl top.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.830
And node itself,
they need metrics.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.260
So this has been
solved by Kubernetes

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.400
as by itself for itself
through something

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.800
that we call Metrics API.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.310
This way, Horizontal
Pod Autoscaling

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.880
can actually work using some
internal metrics on the CPU

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.340
utilization scheduler.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.380
It can make reasonable decisions
on where to place the pods,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:17.880
right.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.585
And kubectl top gets you
the proper information

00:04:20.585 --> 00:04:22.480
on what's really happening.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.290
So the Metrics
API is implemented

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.970
by the Metrics Server.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.910
Metrics Server is technically
scraping the nodes

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.580
in the search for
the metrics data.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.690
That's what's happening there.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.642
So let's have a look at the API.

00:04:40.642 --> 00:04:42.600
And you look at that,
it's super simple, right.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.450
It actually has three components
through the definition.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.610
And one is on the
level of nodes,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.600
the second one is on
the level of pods,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.030
and the third one is just a list
of containers inside of both.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.250
And it really specifies
only two pieces

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.430
of the information, right,
a timestamp, a time window

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.766
and the usage data.

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:02.140
So this is what
we're collecting.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:05.280
This is what the
Metrics API is actually

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.110
getting out of Kubernetes.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.950
Now, let's have a closer look
what's happening in the nodes,

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:12.450
right.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.180
Well, at the nodes, you
obviously have a kubelet.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.990
A kubelet is consulting with
a cAdvisor to scrape data

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.050
from all of the
system components.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.920
Like Docker, like ATCD,
like all other pieces,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.460
like system level pods
that need to be monitored.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.040
And all of that data
is then extracted out

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.770
into the summary API.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:40.950
So through this, well, we have a
system that is self-sufficient.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.040
We call it a Core
Metrics Pipeline.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.300
This is the system that
lets Kubernetes run.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.520
But in order for you to run the
workloads, to run your setup,

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.430
you actually need
more than that,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:00.390
and for this, you need
external solution.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:03.130
We call it third party
applications, right.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.000
These solutions, there
are many of those.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.820
They work in one of two ways.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.600
They either work
in a push model,

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.450
in which the data and metrics
collected gets written directly

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:19.770
to the monitoring solution.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.780
Or they work in a pull model,
where the monitoring solution

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.350
is scraping the data
from the endpoints.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.570
It's hard to say
which one is better.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.190
There are solutions
that work either way,

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.790
and we are going to walk
you through both of these.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.720
So in order to look
at the data, you

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.050
can look at two
possible solutions.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.840
We're going to walk you
here through Prometheus

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:47.630
and Stackdriver.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:50.280
Prometheus works in a
pull mode, Stackdriver

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.200
works in a push mode.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.420
So when you look at the
structure of Kubernetes,

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:03.120
you have the nodes on the bottom
with kubelet, the key component

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.520
of the control of a system.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.750
Then you have pods, and then you
have Kubernetes Master, right.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.880
At the node level, you
have Node Exporter.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.120
Through Node
Exporter, Prometheus

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.920
gets the data of like what's
really happening there,

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.500
CPU utilization,
amount of memory,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.420
all of that kind of set.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.290
And then there is pods.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.840
Pods, as we just
discussed a minute ago,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.850
get the cAdvisor to go
get all of the data of how

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.050
pods are behaving.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:39.510
And on top of that, you have
all of the natural components

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:44.970
of the Kubernetes as a system
that are naturally reported

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.100
in a Prometheus format.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.580
And this is everything
from the Kubernetes API

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:56.130
through the containers' runtime,
kubelet stutters, et cetera.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:01.020
All of these components, in a
very natural and native way,

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.100
get reported by the
Prometheus server that

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.410
sits on each and every node.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:10.740
So now that we
have it, let's have

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.400
a look at how this is
happening in Stackdriver.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.160
So Stackdriver has a
layer of three APIs.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.310
The first one is the
Stackdriving monitoring API.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:23.640
It has a heapster
through which it's

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.200
collecting some of the data.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.660
But in fact, it also has
a Stackdriver Prometheus

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:28.530
collector.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.150
So Stackdriver
Prometheus collector

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.780
is capturing all of the
data that Prometheus

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.970
is scraping from all of
the system components,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.840
like whatever
number, 500 or 200.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.620
So anything that is happening
in the Kubernetes layer

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.876
can now be accessible
through the Stackdriver.

00:08:48.876 --> 00:08:50.500
And then there are
two more components.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.600
One is the metadata API
that writes the data

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:57.360
through an agent
that gets deployed

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.620
on each and every note.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.050
And the same deployment
of a daemon set

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.040
is also deploying
another fault that

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:06.330
is called Stackdriver
logging agent that

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:07.980
is collecting the logs.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.690
So through this,
you have a full set.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.230
This works in a push mode.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.520
And now when you look
at that holistically,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:18.210
you have Prometheus clients
that are on each and every node,

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:20.880
and then you have
two possible layers,

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.840
two possible ways you can
write your metrics data, right.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.670
The top one is the
open source format.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:30.040
The Prometheus collector
gets all the data,

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.250
then it uses own
storage, and you

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.640
can present it either in
Prometheus or in Grafana.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.670
I'm sure if you're using
any on premise solution,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.271
you're very familiar with this.

00:09:40.271 --> 00:09:41.520
And then you have Stackdriver.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.210
Stackdriver does the same,
but it has the Stackdriver

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.640
Prometheus collector, so
it collects all of the data

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.420
from Prometheus.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:52.200
But on top of that, it's also
getting some electric data

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.790
and metadata, right.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.210
And again, you can
say in Stackdriver,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:02.100
you are monitoring UI or
you can graph it in Grafana.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.090
So that's it, that's the story.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.560
And now, for the
rest of the talk,

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.210
I would like to
invite to the stage

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.670
JD Velazquez and
Shoucong Chan, who

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.400
are going to talk about how
we can see it in practice.

00:10:24.905 --> 00:10:25.530
That's for you.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:30.452
JD VELASQUEZ: Thank you, Jerzy.

00:10:30.452 --> 00:10:31.160
Hello, everybody.

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:35.110
So we want to tell you and give
you a demo of how all of this

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:35.920
works.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.020
But before we do that,
let's try to orient

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:39.400
ourselves a little bit.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.160
Before we go to demos, put
on the slides again, please.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:46.092
We'll try to orient ourselves a
little bit on what application

00:10:46.092 --> 00:10:47.800
we have here, to give
you a sense of what

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:49.280
it is that we're doing here.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.840
So this is a simple Java
application some of you

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.140
may be familiar with.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:55.009
It's a pet clinic.

00:10:55.009 --> 00:10:56.050
It's a veterinary clinic.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.760
Who here has pets?

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.070
Come on, a lot more
you have pets, yes?

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.110
But hopefully, your
veterinary clinic

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.870
is not using this application
to handle everything inside

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.036
because it's pretty buggy
and it has all the things,

00:11:08.036 --> 00:11:10.580
but we're going to try to
solve those issues right now.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:13.670
So this is, again, like I
said, just a simple pet clinic.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.060
Imagine that you are an employee
on this veterinary clinic,

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.720
and you want to be able to
handle the visits from all

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:21.790
of your customers.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:24.050
They're pet owners,
they have pets.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.437
And you want to track what
sort of things they're doing,

00:11:26.437 --> 00:11:28.770
the types of visit, the type
of veterinarians they have,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:29.940
and so on.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.660
So to orient ourselves
further in this application,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.670
to really go into a hybrid
scenario, what we've

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.830
implemented here
to give you a sense

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.840
is an architecture like this.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.150
We essentially
have a cluster that

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.580
has been deployed on
prem, Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.370
We have four
microservices here, doing

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:48.950
what they're supposed to
do, managing customers,

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.570
managing the visits, managing
the veterinarians in general.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.470
And the same cluster
we have actually

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.310
deployed on Kubernetes engine on
GCP, and both of those clusters

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:02.660
communicate with the data
source, which is Cloud Spanner.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:04.920
So that's essentially
the app, very simple.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:08.330
Now, let's go to the demo
and we'll see this in action

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.460
specifically.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:13.400
So, and you know, just to orient
ourselves a little bit further

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.990
and start to generate a
little traffic to this app,

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.820
let's go in and
use it just a bit.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.980
Let's list the set of owners
that may exist already.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:24.890
This business may not be doing
very well right now, since it

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:29.420
has no owners or very few.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:30.650
Of course, it's a live demo.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.650
What could go wrong?

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:34.500
There we go.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.010
OK, so we have a
few owners already.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:40.429
What we can do is, let's add a
new pet to one of those owners

00:12:40.429 --> 00:12:42.470
so we get a sense of the
different services, what

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:43.040
they do.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.190
Let's add a new pet to
this particular one.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:47.550
Let's give it a name.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.570
We of course want to know the
date of birth for that pet,

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.250
and then one type of pet.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:58.610
I prefer dogs, but there's
of course, lots of cats.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:12:59.894
Now, we can add visits.

00:12:59.894 --> 00:13:02.060
We see that some of the
pets for this owner, they've

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:03.186
already visited the clinic.

00:13:03.186 --> 00:13:04.184
And then do some things.

00:13:04.184 --> 00:13:06.700
This new pet, we're going to
take him in for the first exam,

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.080
it's a physical
exam, and that's it.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.790
Now we may ask you, of
course, to go in and generate

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.030
some traffic for this,
but instead of doing that,

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:17.510
let us just generate a little
bit of more traffic right now.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.107
The idea here is, of course,
we want to scale up things

00:13:20.107 --> 00:13:22.440
so that we can do some of the
troubleshooting scenarios.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.620
So we just are going
to our on prem cluster,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.270
and as you see here, we're just
executing a scaling command,

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:32.370
and we're just scaling
up that on prem cluster.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.130
Now, we did that on one side.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.140
Let's do it now
on the GK cluster,

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:37.940
on from Kubernetes engine.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:40.410
So let's go to
our cloud console.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:42.480
Let's connect to the cluster.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.610
And when we do that,
we see our cloud shell.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:48.120
