00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:38.760
may be useful for you depending
on the role that you may have.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:42.630
So if you're an SRE and
operater, for instance,

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.142
you may want to look at the
state of your infrastructure.

00:20:45.142 --> 00:20:46.850
You want to make sure
that your nodes are

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.290
healthy and performant.

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:51.240
So that's what you
may be doing here.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.690
But if you're a developer,
you may actually want to be--

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:56.397
and of course we want for you
to focus on writing your code

00:20:56.397 --> 00:20:57.980
and look at your
applications-- so you

00:20:57.980 --> 00:21:00.080
may be more
interested in looking

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:02.360
at it from the perspective
of your deployments

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.430
or your services, particularly.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:07.160
So we can drill down from
a cluster to a namespace

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:08.914
to the services that you want.

00:21:08.914 --> 00:21:10.580
Notice as well that
as we're doing this,

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:12.204
you can actually
drill down all the way

00:21:12.204 --> 00:21:14.630
from your cluster in all of
these views all the way down

