00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:03.140
This is a Java app.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:05.480
So let's look into
one particularly.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:10.380
Let's do the JVM Memory Use
Bytes to get a sense here.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.030
So all right.

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:11.550
There we go.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.357
So lots of signals but
fast and available--

00:23:14.357 --> 00:23:15.940
you get a sense for
what we have here.

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:18.356
Now the good thing is that
we've added a lot of dimensions

00:23:18.356 --> 00:23:19.310
to these metrics.

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:22.490
So you can filter in group
by a lot of metadata,

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:24.972
including Kubernetes labels
and user-defined labels.

00:23:24.972 --> 00:23:26.180
So let's try to do some here.

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:29.300
Let's filter by service name.

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:31.367
And well the service
name we can do--

00:23:31.367 --> 00:23:33.950
notice that you get actually an
autocomplete of all the values

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:35.360
that apply to that label.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:38.390
Let's do the API gateway.

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:39.296
Apply that.

00:23:39.296 --> 00:23:40.670
OK, that's still
a lot of signals

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.230
but a little bit better.

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:44.030
We could still group
I and aggregate

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:47.880
maybe creative 95th
percentile or some sort.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.150
But what we have here is
maybe the easiest thing

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:52.130
